<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Washington-on-the-Brazos</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Washington-on-the-Brazos is a small community on the Brazos River in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> where delegates to the Convention of 1836 declared Texas independent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> on 2 March 1836 and drafted its constitution. It later served as the seat of government of the Republic of Texas in the 1840s. The site is preserved as a state historic site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/washington-on-the-brazos.jpg" alt="Washington-on-the-Brazos" width="320" height="167" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Noconatom · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Independence_Hall,_Washington_on_the_Brazos,_Texas.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
